# Laziness proof: filter returns a Seq; head only forces the first element of that Seq.
# The input dict [1 2 [/ 1 0]] is filtered by [> x 0]. Elements 1 and 2 both satisfy
# the predicate, so element 1 becomes the head of the resulting Seq. head returns after
# finding the first element, so element 3 ([/ 1 0]) — the laziness sentinel — is never
# forced and its division-by-zero error never fires.
#
# The evaluator resolves `filtered` correctly via letrec scoping (both entries share scope).
# The type checker warns "undefined variable: filtered" because its letrec-scope analysis
# does not yet implement cross-binding forward references for multi-entry dicts. The
# warning is a known type checker limitation, not an evaluator bug. The === warn section
# documents and pins this expected diagnostic so unexpected changes are caught.
[filtered: [filter [fn [x] [> x 0]] [1 2 [/ 1 0]]]]
[result: [head filtered]]
=== out
Dict({"result": Int(1)})
=== warn
undefined variable: filtered
